-- title:
--   Gaussian and cyclotomic coefficient rings inside the complex numbers
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a formal proof that a real circulant Hadamard matrix of positive order $n$ exists only for $n=1$ and $n=4$. Such a matrix has entries $\pm1$, satisfies $HH^{\mathsf T}=nI$, and has the form $H_{ij}=h(j-i)$ with indices in $\mathbb Z/n$. Its first row becomes an element $f$ of the integer group ring $\mathbb Z[\mathbb Z/n]$ with $f f^{*}=n$, where $f^{*}$ reverses the indices. Two-adic descent reduces every order $n>1$ to $n=4u^2$ with $u$ odd; for $u>1$, evaluation at characters with values in cyclotomic rings and a local argument at a prime above $2$ give a contradiction.
--
--   1. **Chosen roots.** $\rho_p=e^{2\pi i/p}$, a primitive $p$th root of unity for $p\ne0$, with $\bar\rho_p=\rho_p^{\,p-1}$ and $\rho_p\bar\rho_p=1$.
--   2. **Gaussian integers.** $\mathbb Z[i]\subseteq\mathbb C$, the subring generated by $i$, with its element $i$.
--   3. **Root rings.** For a finite set $s$ of naturals, the subring $\mathbb Z[i,\rho_q:q\in s]$ of $\mathbb C$; for an odd $u$,
--   $$
--   B_u=\mathbb Z\bigl[i,\rho_q:q\mid u\text{ prime}\bigr],
--   $$
--   and the away ring $B_{u,\hat p}$ omits $\rho_p$. These rings are closed under complex conjugation, so they carry a star, and are finitely generated $\mathbb Z$-modules; the inclusions $\mathbb Z[i]\subseteq B_{u,\hat p}\subseteq B_u$ commute with the star.
--   4. **Single generator.** For $s$ a set of primes, $\eta_s=i\prod_{q\in s}\rho_q$ of order $4\prod_{q\in s}q$; for the away ring, $s$ is the prime divisors of $u$ other than $p$.
